    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-01",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-01",
      "dcterms:title": "Reference script output is not reproducible byte for byte",
      "dcterms:description": "QR Code Generation 12 and 13 say the script \"regenerates the example above byte for byte\". It does not. The DSC is signed by the SCA, and the COSE_Sign1 by the DSC, using randomized ECDSA, so every run produces a different DSC, a different kid and different HC1 strings. Running the script at PR head 50bcb25 twice produced kids ZfzK2Csfpsk= and 2sMkNOHFctA=, against WHM+BYqqZiE= in the PR, and different HC1 strings on every run. The committed artefacts are internally consistent: both committed HC1 strings verify against the committed DSC and the kid matches. The sizes in 12.5 are not stable either: the deflated size came out as 283 bytes instead of 282, and the HC1 length as 429 characters instead of 427.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:resolutionHint": "Either use RFC 6979 deterministic ECDSA for both signatures, or reword the claim to \"regenerates an equivalent example\" and treat the committed JSON and PEM as the normative test vector. The library requirements assume the committed artefacts are the vector (STT-TST-001).",
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-12"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-13"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/doc-qr-script"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-02",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-02",
      "dcterms:title": "One payload per hcert, or several?",
      "dcterms:description": "QR Code Generation 2 and 4 say an HCERT carries exactly one payload and that -260 is a \"map whose single key is the subclaim\". HCERT Specification 3.2.7 says \"Several different types of health certificate MAY exist under the same claim\", and the HCert logical model makes every subclaim 0..1, so it does not rule out more than one. The verifier behaviour for several entries is not specified.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:resolutionHint": "State in hcert_spec 3.2.7 whether more than one subclaim per hcert is allowed and how a verifier treats it.",
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/hcert-3.2.7"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/doc-sd-hcert"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-03",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-03",
      "dcterms:title": "EKU certificate type for non-DCC payloads is undefined",
      "dcterms:description": "Appendix A.2 defines EKU OIDs for test, vaccinations and recovery, and QR Code Generation 11 step 7 says to check the EKU \"against the certificate type\". There is no mapping from subclaims 3, 4, 5, -6 or -7 to a certificate type. DVC (-6) is presumably \"vaccinations\". VHL (5) has no obvious type.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:resolutionHint": "Add a subclaim-to-certificate-type table, or use the GDHCN Key Usage value set.",
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-11"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/hcert-A.2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-04",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-04",
      "dcterms:title": "Base45 reference points at an expired Internet-Draft",
      "dcterms:description": "Both pages cite draft-faltstrom-base45. That draft was published as RFC 9285, which should be the reference.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-8"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/hcert-4.2.2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/std-base45-draft"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/std-rfc9285"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-05",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-05",
      "dcterms:title": "CBOR reference RFC 7049 is obsoleted by RFC 8949",
      "dcterms:description": "Neither page says whether issuers should use deterministic encoding, or which rules. The reference script uses length-first canonical ordering (RFC 7049 section 3.9). For the integer and short text keys used here that gives the same order as RFC 8949 core deterministic encoding, but that is luck, not a rule.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/hcert-3.2.7"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/std-rfc7049"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/std-rfc8949"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-06",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-06",
      "dcterms:title": "Subclaim 5 is called VHL in one place and SHL in another",
      "dcterms:description": "QR Code Generation 2.1 and the HCert logical model describe subclaim 5 as the URI of a Verifiable Health Link. HCERT Specification 3.2.7.1.5, which is the authoritative list, describes it as Smart Health Link (SHL) and links the SMART Health Links spec. The two define different URI schemes and payloads.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-2.1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/hcert-3.2.7.1.5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/doc-sd-hcert"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-07",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-07",
      "dcterms:title": "iss is a string in the spec but a Coding in the logical model",
      "dcterms:description": "The CWTPayload logical model types claim 1 as Coding. HCERT 3.2.4 and RFC 8392 type it as a string.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/hcert-3.2.4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/doc-sd-cwtpayload"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-08",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-08",
      "dcterms:title": "Logical model link in hcert_spec uses a different case",
      "dcterms:description": "hcert_spec.md links StructureDefinition-hcert.html. The resource id is HCert, so the page is StructureDefinition-HCert.html, which is the link QR Code Generation 2.1 uses. On case-sensitive hosting the lowercase link breaks.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/hcert-3.2.1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/hcert-3.2.7.1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/doc-sd-hcert"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-09",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-09",
      "dcterms:title": "DVC subclaim -6 is in the development range",
      "dcterms:description": "The number \"is not guaranteed to be stable\". The library must not bake -6 into its wire format, and has to be able to move DVC to a WHO-assigned number without a breaking API change.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": false,
      "stt:concerns": {
        "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-2.1"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-10",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-10",
      "dcterms:title": "Deployed section anchors and preview paths need checking",
      "dcterms:description": "The ci and published URLs for PR 129 content, and every section anchor, are computed (see sources.yaml) and cannot be checked until the PR is merged and deployed. Re-run tools/build_kg.py --check-links after deployment.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": false,
      "stt:concerns": {
        "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/doc-qr"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-12",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-12",
      "dcterms:title": "Base64 variant of kid in the DID trustlist is unspecified",
      "dcterms:description": "The DID trustlist uses a \"base64 encoded key id\" as the verification method fragment and as JWK kid. It does not say whether that is standard or URL-safe base64, or whether padding is kept. Standard and URL-safe base64 differ on '+' and '/', and the PR example kid WHM+BYqqZiE= contains '+'.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/did-examples"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/hcert-A.1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-13",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-13",
      "dcterms:title": "Mapping between GDHCN participant codes (alpha-3) and iss (alpha-2)",
      "dcterms:description": "Participant codes are ISO 3166-1 alpha-3, while iss and the X.509 C attribute use alpha-2. The test pair XXA/XX is user-assigned and not a formal ISO mapping. A verifier narrowing trust-list lookups by iss needs an authoritative mapping.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-12.3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/hcert-3.2.4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/doc-vs-participants"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-14",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-14",
      "dcterms:title": "Package coordinates and licence for the libraries",
      "dcterms:description": "Maven group / Kotlin package root and crate names are placeholders. The natural reverse-DNS root, int.who, cannot be used because `int` is a reserved Java keyword. The licence has not been chosen.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": false,
      "stt:concerns": {
        "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/dir-languages"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-15",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-15",
      "dcterms:title": "Asynchronous crypto and I/O across the WIT boundary",
      "dcterms:description": "WASI 0.2 component calls are synchronous. WebCrypto (the natural crypto backend for Kotlin/JS on Node and browsers) is promise-based, as are remote KMS signers and HTTP fetches. The design avoids async callbacks into the host: signing is two-phase and trust lists are loaded by the host. The Rust component verifies with in-WASM RustCrypto, so verify stays synchronous. Kotlin/JS must either use Node's synchronous `crypto` module (Node only) or expose a Promise-returning verify, which would diverge from the jco surface. Decide before implementation; revisit when WASI 0.3 async is stable.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": false,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/std-wit"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/std-webcrypto"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-16",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-16",
      "dcterms:title": "WIT type-system limits",
      "dcterms:description": "WIT has no recursive types, so generic CBOR crosses as encoded bytes and only DVCMin and VHL are typed records. s64 becomes bigint in JavaScript, so it is used only for NumericDate, and subclaim numbers are s32. result<T, E> is thrown as ComponentError by jco, so APIs whose failures are expected (verify) return a variant instead.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": false,
      "stt:concerns": {
        "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/std-wit"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-17",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-17",
      "dcterms:title": "The example issues an abstract logical model",
      "dcterms:description": "In smart-trust-phw, DVCMin and DVCMinVaccineDetails are declared abstract (^abstract = true). Only DVCMinPreQual and DVCMinVaccineDetailsPreQual are concrete. The HCert logical model puts DVCMin at subclaim -6, and the PR 129 example issues a DVCMin with ICD-11 codes. So no concrete model exists for a non-PreQual DVC: either DVCMin should not be abstract, or a concrete ICD-11 profile is needed.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/ext-dvcmin"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/ext-dvcmin-vx"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/ext-dvcmin-prequal"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/qr-12.4"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-18",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-18",
      "dcterms:title": "Sex binding in DVCMin points at a CodeSystem, not a ValueSet",
      "dcterms:description": "DVCMin.s is bound (extensible) to $GENDER, which smart-trust-phw aliases to http://hl7.org/fhir/administrative-gender, the CodeSystem URL. A binding must reference a ValueSet: http://hl7.org/fhir/ValueSet/administrative-gender.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/ext-dvcmin"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/ext-phw-aliases"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-19",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-19",
      "dcterms:title": "Upstream home for the CDDL and JSON-LD artefacts",
      "dcterms:description": "The HCERT envelope CDDL (api/cddl/hcert.cddl) belongs in WorldHealthOrganization/smart-trust next to the HCERT Specification, which owns the wire format. The payload JSON-LD context, SHACL and CDDL belong with their logical models in smart-trust-phw, ideally generated by the IG build and published at the canonical URL. They are drafted here until upstream adopts them.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": true,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/hcert-3.2.1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/ext-dvcmin"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/OI-20",
      "@type": "stt:OpenIssue",
      "dcterms:identifier": "OI-20",
      "dcterms:title": "CDDL validator deviations (cddl-rs / pycddl)",
      "dcterms:description": "The only Python CDDL validator, pycddl (wrapping the Rust cddl crate), deviates from RFC 8610 in four ways found here: .cbor inside arrays is applied to the wrong items; .regexp is not implicitly anchored; escaped regex classes such as \\s are treated as literals; and map closure is not enforced across group-choice alternatives. The CDDL files stay spec-correct, and tools/check_models.py works around the first three. The schema avoids relying on the fourth. Cross-check with a second validator (e.g. cddlc) before upstreaming.",
      "stt:upstreamFix": false,
      "stt:concerns": [
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/std-rfc8610"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/std-rfc9682"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/smart-trust-tools/kg/id/STT-GEN-001",
      "@type": "stt:Requirement",
      "dcterms:identifier": "STT-GEN-001",
      "dcterms:title": "Issuance and verification of the full HCERT QR pipeline",
      "stt:statement": "The library SHALL implement issuance (steps 1 to 9) and verification (QR Code Generation section 11) of HCERT QR codes.",
      "stt:conformance": "SHALL",
      "stt:category": "scope",
